from lib import *
C=10;S='maths';CH='numbers-and-sequences'
lesson(C,S,CH,'euclids-division-algorithm-and-fta',"Euclid's division lemma, algorithm and the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic","Sharing sweets","You have 17 sweets and want to pack them 5 to a box. You fill 3 boxes and 2 are left over. That simple fact, 17 = 5 x 3 + 2, is the heart of a very old and powerful idea.",
["Euclid's division lemma: for positive integers a and b there are unique whole numbers q and r with a = bq + r and 0 <= r < b. @ a = bq + r",
 "Here q is the quotient and r the remainder, and r is always smaller than b. @ 0 <= r < b",
 "Euclid's division algorithm uses this lemma repeatedly to find the HCF (highest common factor) of two numbers. @ HCF",
 "To find the HCF of 455 and 42: 455 = 42 x 10 + 35, then 42 = 35 x 1 + 7, then 35 = 7 x 5 + 0. The last non-zero remainder, 7, is the HCF. @ last non-zero remainder",
 "The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every composite number can be written as a product of primes in exactly one way, apart from the order. @ unique prime factorisation",
 "For example 360 = 2 x 2 x 2 x 3 x 3 x 5 = 2^3 x 3^2 x 5. @ 360",
 "Using prime factorisation, HCF takes the smallest powers of the common primes and LCM takes the greatest powers of all primes. @ HCF smallest, LCM greatest",
 "For two numbers, HCF x LCM = product of the two numbers. @ HCF x LCM = a x b"],
("Find the HCF of 135 and 225 by Euclid's algorithm.",[("Divide the larger by the smaller: 225 = 135 x 1 + 90.","Start with a = 225, b = 135."),("Now 135 = 90 x 1 + 45.","Replace the pair by (135, 90)."),("Then 90 = 45 x 2 + 0. The remainder is 0, so the HCF is 45.","The last non-zero remainder is the HCF.")]),
("Explain how Euclid's algorithm finds the HCF and what the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says.",["a = bq + r with r less than b","Repeat until remainder is zero","Every composite number has a unique prime factorisation"],["Taking the zero remainder as the HCF"]),
["a = bq + r with 0 <= r < b.","Euclid's algorithm: repeat division; last non-zero remainder is the HCF.","Every composite number = product of primes, uniquely.","HCF x LCM = product of the two numbers."],
["m|By Euclid's lemma, 17 = 5 x 3 + r gives r =|2|3~The remainder must satisfy 5 x 3 + r = 17;5~Remainder is less than 5;12~Subtract 15 from 17|r = 17 - 15 = 2.",
 "n|HCF of 12 and 18 is|6|12 = 2^2 x 3 and 18 = 2 x 3^2; common smallest powers give 2 x 3 = 6.",
 "t|The remainder in a = bq + r can be equal to b.|False|The remainder must be smaller than b.",
 "n|If HCF(8, 12) = 4, then LCM(8, 12) =|24|LCM = 8 x 12 / 4 = 24.",
 "m|The prime factorisation of 60 is|2 x 2 x 3 x 5|2 x 3 x 10~10 is not prime;4 x 15~Neither is prime;2 x 5 x 6~6 is not prime|60 = 2^2 x 3 x 5.",
 "n|Using Euclid's algorithm, HCF(48, 18) =|6|48 = 18x2+12, 18 = 12x1+6, 12 = 6x2+0.",
 "t|Every composite number has more than one prime factorisation.|False|Apart from order it is unique.",
 "n|If a = 4q + r and the remainder is r = 3 when a = 23, the quotient q is|5|23 = 4 x 5 + 3."])
lesson(C,S,CH,'modular-arithmetic',"Modular arithmetic - clock arithmetic","The clock that wraps","It is 9 o'clock now. What time will it be 5 hours later? Not 14 o'clock on a 12-hour clock - it is 2. The clock wraps around, and that wrapping is modular arithmetic.",
["Let a, b be integers and m a positive integer. We say a is congruent to b modulo m, written a = b (mod m), if m divides a - b. @ congruence",
 "So 17 = 5 (mod 12), because 12 divides 17 - 5 = 12. @ 17 = 5 (mod 12)",
 "Another way: a and b leave the same remainder when divided by m. @ same remainder",
 "To find a (mod m), divide a by m and take the remainder: 100 (mod 7) = 2 because 100 = 7 x 14 + 2. @ remainder",
 "Congruences can be added and multiplied: if a = b and c = d (mod m) then a + c = b + d and ac = bd (mod m). @ add and multiply",
 "Negative numbers also work: -3 = 4 (mod 7), because 7 divides -3 - 4 = -7. @ negative numbers",
 "Real uses: days of the week (mod 7), clocks (mod 12 or 24) and check digits in bar codes. @ uses"],
("Find the remainder when 2^5 x 7 is divided by 5, i.e. compute 2^5 x 7 (mod 5).",[("2^5 = 32 and 32 = 2 (mod 5), because 32 - 2 = 30.","Reduce each factor first."),("7 = 2 (mod 5).","7 - 2 = 5, divisible by 5."),("So the product = 2 x 2 = 4 (mod 5). The remainder is 4.","Multiply the reduced numbers.")]),
("What does a = b (mod m) mean? Give an example with a clock.",["m divides a - b","Same remainder on division by m","Clock wraps around"],["Thinking it means a equals b"]),
["a = b (mod m) means m divides a - b.","a mod m is the remainder when a is divided by m.","Congruences can be added and multiplied.","Used for clocks, calendars and check digits."],
["m|52 = ? (mod 12)|4|5~52 = 12 x 4 + 4;2~Divide 52 by 12 and take the remainder|52 = 48 + 4.",
 "t|17 = 5 (mod 6) is true.|True|17 - 5 = 12 and 6 divides 12.",
 "n|Find 100 mod 7.|2|100 = 7 x 14 + 2.",
 "n|If today is Monday, which day number (0 = Monday) is it after 100 days? Give 100 mod 7.|2|Remainder 2 means Wednesday.",
 "m|-3 = ? (mod 7) between 0 and 6|4|3~-3 + 7 = 4;-4~Add a multiple of 7|-3 + 7 = 4.",
 "t|If a = b (mod m) then a + c = b + c (mod m).|True|You can add the same number to both sides.",
 "n|Find 8 x 9 (mod 5).|2|72 = 5 x 14 + 2.",
 "s|On a 12-hour clock 10 + 5 = ? (give the clock time)|3|15 = 3 (mod 12)."])
